00:37:03.530 --> 00:37:07.200
Real quick, we can just
do what I call messages.

00:37:17.150 --> 00:37:18.525
And that, again,
that's an async.

00:37:21.420 --> 00:37:23.435
And let's call that--

00:37:26.050 --> 00:37:27.928
and we have to get
rid of the Subscribe.

00:37:44.550 --> 00:37:47.180
Let's go ahead--
that's printing out.

00:37:49.760 --> 00:38:01.970
So let's go and
modify some values,

00:38:01.970 --> 00:38:03.860
and you'll see that
update in Realtime.

00:38:14.810 --> 00:38:22.175
And that's really the benefit of
making these observable-based,

00:38:22.175 --> 00:38:26.120
is that's actually doing
the binding to the view.

00:38:26.120 --> 00:38:28.490
The Firestore or the
Realtime Database

00:38:28.490 --> 00:38:31.700
is actually keeping
that connection open.

00:38:31.700 --> 00:38:35.270
If I wanted to just get it once,
there's ways of doing that.

00:38:35.270 --> 00:38:38.900
I could just do a Get
instead of a Subscribe.

00:38:38.900 --> 00:38:44.780
But by default,
this is a good way

00:38:44.780 --> 00:38:46.650
of interacting with
their database.

00:38:46.650 --> 00:38:48.870
Now, what I'm doing here
is this is value changes,

00:38:48.870 --> 00:38:50.990
so this is unique
to AngularFire.

00:38:50.990 --> 00:38:55.310
So the value changes is
just getting me the raw base

00:38:55.310 --> 00:38:59.330
objects out of the database.

00:38:59.330 --> 00:39:01.060
And then when the
user's logged out,

00:39:01.060 --> 00:39:03.080
we return this empty array.

00:39:03.080 --> 00:39:05.080
And the cool thing here
is this is automatically

00:39:05.080 --> 00:39:09.760
going to clean up all of
our prior subscriptions.

00:39:09.760 --> 00:39:12.910
So this is one of the powers
of reactive programming.

00:39:12.910 --> 00:39:18.760
I'm doing the switch map, and
if I were writing this just

00:39:18.760 --> 00:39:22.190
by myself, I'd have
to do a lot of IFs.

00:39:22.190 --> 00:39:23.510
I'd have to do a lot of ELSEs.

00:39:23.510 --> 00:39:26.830
I'd have to cancel
subscriptions.

00:39:26.830 --> 00:39:28.964
That's a lot of
work, and anywhere

00:39:28.964 --> 00:39:31.130
where you have a condition,
you're bound to mess up.

00:39:36.900 --> 00:39:40.260
So I'm going to cheat here.

00:39:40.260 --> 00:39:44.540
Running a little low on time,
so I'll pull my Dart code.

00:40:01.720 --> 00:40:03.630
And of course,
there's a conflict.

00:40:15.300 --> 00:40:18.680
So I made a small
modification here.

00:40:18.680 --> 00:40:24.350
I'm actually now
swapping out the body.

00:40:24.350 --> 00:40:25.240
Let's see here.

00:40:29.670 --> 00:40:33.990
And I want the incoming change.

00:40:33.990 --> 00:40:38.850
So I'm swapping out the body
for a message list component.

00:40:38.850 --> 00:40:43.120
The message list is
now this block of code.

00:40:43.120 --> 00:40:45.720
So this is a stateless
widget, all the states

00:40:45.720 --> 00:40:47.190
held in Firestore.

00:40:47.190 --> 00:40:51.630
So I don't care if a
reload destroys it.

00:40:51.630 --> 00:40:55.200
I'm actually
initializing Firestore.

00:40:55.200 --> 00:40:57.750
This is the build
method, so whenever

00:40:57.750 --> 00:41:00.090
this gets instantiated,
it's going to call the build

00:41:00.090 --> 00:41:03.070
and this is going to
draw the components.

00:41:03.070 --> 00:41:04.960
So this is a stream.

00:41:04.960 --> 00:41:10.560
This is Flutters,
like an observable.

00:41:10.560 --> 00:41:13.370
It's a stream of
values, an observable.

00:41:13.370 --> 00:41:17.310
And it's an observable
of query snapshots.

00:41:17.310 --> 00:41:21.240
So the stream source here,
that's this argument,

00:41:21.240 --> 00:41:28.230
is coming from the Firestore
messages, where it proved true.

00:41:28.230 --> 00:41:31.230
Firestore collection
messages, where proved true.

00:41:31.230 --> 00:41:33.637
And if you're familiar
with the Realtime Database,

00:41:33.637 --> 00:41:35.220
one of the cool
things about Firestore

00:41:35.220 --> 00:41:39.580
is I can append more
WHERE conditions on here,

00:41:39.580 --> 00:41:42.560
and they're all hands.

00:41:42.560 --> 00:41:45.250
We can pull in the
snapshots from that.

00:41:45.250 --> 00:41:48.330
And if it has data--

00:41:48.330 --> 00:41:50.040
if it does not have
data, it's loading.

00:41:50.040 --> 00:41:54.570
So let's put a text widget
in there that says loading.

00:41:54.570 --> 00:41:58.720
Let's get the count and
then create a ListView.

00:41:58.720 --> 00:42:02.070
So this is really
common in programming

00:42:02.070 --> 00:42:06.960
with Firebase, whether it
be Flutter, JavaScript, iOS,

00:42:06.960 --> 00:42:10.890
Android, you actually
include your drawing

00:42:10.890 --> 00:42:14.550
code inside that callback.

00:42:14.550 --> 00:42:16.890
You can do some performance
tweaking on top of it,

00:42:16.890 --> 00:42:20.730
but the idea is that whenever
that callback triggers,

00:42:20.730 --> 00:42:24.660
its because data in the database
changed that you care about.

00:42:24.660 --> 00:42:27.930
So let's redraw the list.

00:42:27.930 --> 00:42:29.680
So this is now the ItemBuilder.

00:42:29.680 --> 00:42:32.370
So we send it the count.

00:42:32.370 --> 00:42:36.120
This would be just like a
RecyclerView in Android,

00:42:36.120 --> 00:42:39.780
or ListView on iOS.

00:42:39.780 --> 00:42:41.490
And we have this builder.

00:42:41.490 --> 00:42:44.760
We grab the document's snapshot.

00:42:44.760 --> 00:42:50.490
And we'll do a title from
the document to message,

00:42:50.490 --> 00:42:53.260
and then the subtitle
from the FROM name.

00:42:53.260 --> 00:42:57.995
So let's go ahead and
save that and reload.

00:43:05.590 --> 00:43:06.390
So there.

00:43:06.390 --> 00:43:07.095
We see the wow.

00:43:20.970 --> 00:43:32.660
Just like on Web, if I go
in here and alter things,

00:43:32.660 --> 00:43:34.100
it updates in Realtime.

00:43:43.550 --> 00:43:46.640
So we didn't get very far
in building our application,

00:43:46.640 --> 00:43:49.610
but we got interacting
with the Firestore.

00:43:49.610 --> 00:43:54.320
We got interacting with
authentication on the Web.

00:43:54.320 --> 00:43:57.950
But we have some next steps.

00:43:57.950 --> 00:44:01.590
We could use FCM for
our push notifications.

00:44:01.590 --> 00:44:04.850
So the idea with FCM
is you can register

00:44:04.850 --> 00:44:06.320
for those push notifications.

00:44:06.320 --> 00:44:07.910
It gives you a token.

00:44:07.910 --> 00:44:12.290
You could then call that
token against the admin SDK

00:44:12.290 --> 00:44:16.370
and be able to send a push
notification-- iOS, Android,

00:44:16.370 --> 00:44:19.100
and the web, web
browsers that support it.

00:44:19.100 --> 00:44:24.500
That's the reason why I didn't
build the Angular app as a PWA.

00:44:24.500 --> 00:44:28.520
I showed the Flutter
is particularly on iOS.

00:44:28.520 --> 00:44:32.840
Safari doesn't
allow Web Push yet.

00:44:32.840 --> 00:44:35.270
The app that I'm building,
we wanted integration

00:44:35.270 --> 00:44:36.350
with our LDAP.

00:44:36.350 --> 00:44:39.090
I'll touch on that in a second.

00:44:39.090 --> 00:44:41.070
We need to add the
security rules, of course,

00:44:41.070 --> 00:44:43.400
to make sure that
our data is secure.

00:44:43.400 --> 00:44:46.520
And then finally,
with the Flutter app,

00:44:46.520 --> 00:44:49.980
we can build production
versions of our application,

00:44:49.980 --> 00:44:51.740
but we need to sign those.

00:44:51.740 --> 00:44:53.960
So you can sign
those using Fastlane.

00:44:53.960 --> 00:44:55.670
That's a tool that
I like to use.

00:44:55.670 --> 00:44:59.240
Open source, it allows you to
log in to the Apple Developer

00:44:59.240 --> 00:45:03.470
Portal, pull the credentials,
sign the application,

00:45:03.470 --> 00:45:04.310
all built in Ruby.

00:45:04.310 --> 00:45:06.250
It's a cool script.

00:45:06.250 --> 00:45:08.690
And we can deploy
that to an MDM server

00:45:08.690 --> 00:45:10.700
if we're doing an
internal deployment.

00:45:10.700 --> 00:45:15.290
And that formula allows
you to build, especially

00:45:15.290 --> 00:45:21.080
for an enterprise app, internal
use very, very quickly.

00:45:21.080 --> 00:45:24.890
If you're interested in
actually deploying your own app,

00:45:24.890 --> 00:45:30.510
seeing how you could integrate
with Legacy endpoints, or SOAP

00:45:30.510 --> 00:45:35.480
APIs, or do some extra cool
stuff, our boot camp on Friday,

00:45:35.480 --> 00:45:39.840
Firebase plus Apigee, so
Apigee being the API portal.

00:45:39.840 --> 00:45:43.220
And we'll do some cool
